## Why the GPL rather than a permissive license

Two of the source files are **generated from the Transmission source tree** and are therefore
derivative works of it:

| Generated file | Derived from |
|:--|:--|
| `transmission_sdk/src/main/ets/compat/LegacyKeys.ets` | `libtransmission/api-compat.cc`, `libtransmission/quark.{h,cc}` |
| `transmission_sdk/src/main/ets/constants/RpcFields.ets` | `docs/rpc-spec.md` |
| `transmission_sdk/src/main/ets/model/SessionSetRequest.ets` | `docs/rpc-spec.md` |

Transmission is Copyright © Mnemosyne LLC and is itself dual-licensed under GPL-2.0-only or
GPL-3.0-only. Applying a permissive license to those generated tables would misrepresent their
provenance, so the whole SDK carries the same dual license.

The generators in `scripts/` only *read* the Transmission checkout; they do not copy code into the
build at run time. Everything else in this repository — the transport port, the RPC engine, the
compatibility layer's logic, the models, the tests and the sample application — was written for this
project.

## Note for application developers

If your HarmonyOS application cannot be distributed under the GPL, you have two options:

1. Re-derive the two tables from the RPC specification yourself. `docs/rpc-spec.md` (the protocol
   documentation) is a specification, not code, and the mechanical mappings (`percent_done` →
   `percentDone`, …) can be written independently; only the C++ table transcription is derivative.
   `scripts/generate-legacy-keys.mjs` documents exactly which rows are needed.
2. Talk to the Transmission maintainers (Mnemosyne LLC) about the terms that apply to your use; the
   upstream project already offers an "or any future license endorsed by Mnemosyne LLC" option on its
   files.

This file is a plain-language summary, not legal advice.
